<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=oracle-bone-script">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oracle bone script</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1250 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a> · Also known as Jiaguwen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The oracle bone script is the oldest attested form of written Chinese, carved mostly on ox shoulder bones and turtle plastrons for the Late Shang royal family from the 13th century BCE; inscriptions of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-ding">Wu Ding</a>'s reign are radiocarbon dated to 1254–1197 BCE. Most of the estimated 150,000 inscriptions, found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yinxu">Yinxu</a>, the last Shang capital, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anyang">Anyang</a>, record divinations. Already a mature system, with phonetic loans and phono-semantic compounds, it is the direct ancestor of the later Chinese scripts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oracle-bone-script.jpg" alt="Oracle bone script" width="320" height="335" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shang_Inscribed_Ox_Scapula_(for_divination).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 1250 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Anyang, China</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/empire/shang-dynasty">Shang dynasty</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Its mature form implies earlier development, but no older writing has been found.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=oracle-bone-script">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
